  data:  # 2d5b
    kind: work_item  # 2d5b
    title: "taint.py: TaintSource data model lacks attribute-access form, blocks env-secret and stdio-read threat models (companion to WI-guhok)"  # 2d5b
    status: todo_hard  # 2d5b
    priority: 2  # 2d5b
    parent:  # 2d5b
    tags:  # 2d5b
    - analysis_quality  # 2d5b
    isbefore: []  # 2d5b
    duplicate_of: []  # 2d5b
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 2d5b
    pr_ref:  # 2d5b
    description: "Filed during self-analysis dogfooding 2026-04-21 while investigating whether WI-guhok propagates to the taint analyzer.\n\nSummary: The taint catalog cannot express attribute-style primitives as taint sources. os.environ['SECRET'], sys.argv, sys.path — none of these can be declared as sources in any taint_sources/*.yaml file, because TaintSource's data model doesn't accept that shape. This blocks any host-secrets-exfiltration threat model from being validated by verify-claims.\n\nEvidence:\n\n1. packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/taint.py TaintSource dataclass fields:\n     taint_label: str\n     module: str\n     name: str\n     kind: str  # 'function' or 'method' — NOT 'attribute'\n     return_tainted: bool = True\n     argument_tainted: tuple[int, ...] = ()\n   There is no attribute-access form. The 'kind' discriminator is hardcoded to function|method.\n\n2. Compare io_primitives/python.yaml which DOES support attribute access (the feature WI-guhok covers for the evaluator side, see env_read: - module: os; attributes: [environ]).\n\n3. ADR-0017 never discusses attribute-style sources. The omission is undocumented, which supports the hypothesis that it's an oversight from taint.py being written after io_primitives without carrying that feature over, rather than a deliberate design rejection.\n\nWhy this matters for the security audit:\n\n- 39 os.environ / os.getenv call sites exist across hypergumbo's own Python code.\n- Credential-file reads (.env, ~/.aws/credentials, ~/.ssh/id_rsa, ~/.netrc, git credential helpers) are also common sensitive sources. These may be expressible as function calls (open('/home/user/.ssh/id_rsa')) but the pattern 'open called with a path matching SENSITIVE_PATHS' is also not expressible in the current TaintSource schema — the schema matches by module.function name, not by argument value.\n- Reading env vars is exactly the kind of 'sensitive source that could reach a normally-benign sink' that motivated the user to ask the audit question in the first place.\n\nRelation to other items:\n\n- WI-guhok covers the evaluator-side bug in the io_primitives pipeline (io-boundaries rule-evaluator ignoring attributes: entries).\n- WI-hizik-... covers drift between built-in io_primitives sinks and built-in taint_sinks.\n- THIS item covers the taint_sources side: extending the TaintSource data model and YAML schema to accept attribute-style primitives, so env reads can be declared as taint sources at all.\n\nScope of fix (larger than a YAML edit):\n\n1. Extend TaintSource dataclass to accept kind='attribute' (or add a parallel attributes: list on the per-module entry, matching the io_primitives YAML shape).\n2. Update the YAML loader in taint.py to parse attributes: entries.\n3. Update the taint propagation pass to recognize attribute-access AST nodes (subscript, attribute-lookup) as matching sources. This may require touching the def/use extractor path (ADR-0017 §110+) depending on how the structural matcher is wired today.\n4. Add a built-in taint_sources/host_env.yaml with entries for os.environ attribute access, os.getenv, sys.argv, etc., labeled taint_label: host_secret (new label).\n5. Add corresponding built-in sinks if missing (taint_sinks/host_filesystem.yaml covers fs writes, taint_sinks/network_send.yaml covers net sends — those should be the right sinks for host_secret).\n6. Tests: a fixture with os.environ read flowing to open().write() should be flagged as a violation; a fixture where the env value is sanitized (e.g. hash or truncation) should not.\n\nNot a blocker for the current dogfooding audit (which uses manual inspection of io-boundaries chains), but is the structural fix that would let verify-claims answer the host-secrets-exfiltration question automatically on future runs. Also blocks any taint-based README/safety claim of the form 'we checked automatically'."  # 2d5b
    fields: {}  # 2d5b
